(5) IPPS-A: expert in system navigation, functionality, task execution, and execution of HR business processes in the system.', '602bd0df34804eb1cce609542b1c905d9b90e474b03f9c2297d12acac015feb1', 4, 5, null, 11, null, null, false, null),
  ('caeb4403-82fd-5917-97c2-c15e17ac224c', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Career Management. 42R Soldiers and NCOs should be enrolled in and actively utilizing Army Career Tracker (ACT) as a tool to monitor and guide career progression both for themselves and their subordinates. In addition, NCOs should ensure that their subordinates are enrolled in and understand the functions of the ACT. The ACT, in conjunction with DA PAM 600- 25, will provide Soldiers and leaders with the information and guidance needed to maximize career progression opportunities for Soldiers throughout all stages of their career. Army Musicians who want to be competitive for promotion to the senior ranks should seek assignments at different types of bands in different locations (Direct Support and General Support, Operating Force and Generating Force, CONUS and OCONUS) as well as broadening assignments. The most competitive Soldiers will have a record of service in several Music Support Sections, musical and troop leadership roles, and collateral duty positions as described in the following paragraphs.
(2) Achievements. Recognition for individual achievement through the course of a Soldier’s career can contribute to being recognized above their peers for advancement.
(a) Army Musicians may be nominated for Adjutant General’s Corps and/or Regimental awards which include the Army Band Soldier and NCO of the Year, AG Corps Soldier and NCO of the Year, Colonel Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, Major General Horatio Gates Medal (Bronze/Gold), Colonel Robert L. Manning Achievement Medal, President Benjamin Harrison Medal, Sergeant Major Larry L. Strickland Medal, and the President Theodore Roosevelt Medal.
(b) Personal Awards. Recognition of exemplary performance can help to set a Soldier apart from their peers for promotion consideration and should be evaluated based upon the individual events and merit that resulted in recognition. Awards should not serve as a sole discriminator for promotion consideration.
(c) Recognition. Soldiers selected for membership in the Sergeant Morales or Sergeant Audie Murphy Clubs, as well as awards from BDE or higher Soldier of the Quarter/NCO of the Quarter or Soldier of the Year/NCO of the Year competition boards should be considered favorably in comparison to their peers. Soldiers and NCOs who are named in the top 20% or 21-40% at NCOES have set themselves apart from their peers.', '012195242d93b09ff76fe963efaa0fbda8c0f91e76e257ec9a40b2956812dc0d', 15, 16, null, 12, null, null, false, null),
  ('6af65a6c-9919-56b2-82bd-1ec4ce11ac8b', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'assignments', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Specialist/Corporal — Assignments', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'The focus during the early years of an Army Musician’s career must be on building a strong base of technical expertise in basic MOS skills and common Soldier tasks. A Musician (instrumentalist, vocalist, or music support technician) performs confidently as a member of an MPT in all applicable styles and settings as directed by unit mission. In addition to their musical duties Musicians may be assigned as clerks or assistants in support of their band’s Music Support Sections.
(1) Operational Assignments. Assignments include Musician at a General Support or Direct Support Band.
(2) Institutional Assignments. None
(3) Broadening Assignments. Musician (SHAPE International Band)
(4) Key and Developmental Assignments. Musician', 'a4cae28e84796cc369a924409553e94d2a93e37e6b791820c1abb1affb420f4f', 16, 16, null, 13, null, null, false, null),
  ('5ea62af8-ce0c-5768-9fe4-3a3d7bee6b74', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'training', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Specialist/Corporal — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Professional Military Education. Basic Leader Course (BLC). Consider Soldiers ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve recognition such as Honor Graduate, Commandant’s List, Distinguished Leader Award, or Superior Academic Achievement.
(2) JPME. N/A
(3) Functional Training. Functional courses enhance the effectiveness of military personnel in specific skill areas. These courses do not award an MOS, SQI, or ASI, but the skills are important to overall unit readiness. Examples include CBRNE defense, unit armorer, ammunition handler, combat life saver, DTMS operator, EST operator, field sanitation, unit movement officer, unit load team, HAZMAT transportation, air load planner, container control officer, self-help facilities maintenance, GCSS-A supply maintenance manager, equal opportunity leader (EOL), unit retention NCO, and unit prevention leader (UPL).
(4) Additional Training. Additional skills qualification training. NCOs may be selected for ASI and SQI producing courses that are necessary for overall unit readiness and training or for specific assignments: Air Assault (ASI 2B) and Airborne (SQI P).', '51cf94160646fda2811c107ba691de0b4c562007a3d34c95755c8d26faa21e31', 16, 16, null, 14, null, null, false, null),
  ('a779a94c-4113-5d26-911f-933d65ad5edf', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Specialist/Corporal — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Civilian Education. Many band members enlist after completing advanced degrees in their musical performance or technical specialty. Continued study in any discipline is highly encouraged regardless of degrees and certifications earned before enlistment.
(a) Soldiers can receive up to $4500 per fiscal year through the Army’s tuition and credentialing assistance programs (https://www.armyignited.army.mil)
(b) The Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) provides no-cost education and career-planning, including college credit by examination or for military training and experiences. https://www.dantes.mil.', '9fcc9f2a5a0b8863bf0a94523d1db277f69c2d5ad265b4c31d6d4c205b136016', 16, 17, null, 15, null, null, false, null),
  ('7a43d76b-c257-5656-970d-dccb082898e4', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'self_development', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Specialist/Corporal — Self-Development', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Competitive Boards. Competitive Soldier boards such as Soldier of the Quarter/Year broaden the knowledge base, instill discipline, and improve the Soldier’s ability to communicate verbally.
(2) Stewardship of the profession. The Center for Army Leadership conducts studies, develops doctrine, provides assessment programs, and develops products and services that improve Army leadership, support developing leaders and strengthen the profession in order to achieve positive command climates and mission ready units. Visit https://usacac.army.mil/Organizations/Centers-of-Excellence-CoE/MCCoE/Center-For-Army- Leadership-CAL/ for self and leader development tools and kits.
(3) Guided. Musicians must work to achieve or exceed MOSQ standards stated in USASOM Regulation 350-70-4 in order to maintain MOS qualification and continually strive to improve their MOS skills to meet the technical and leadership requirements of higher grades. The most qualified Army Musicians should have a 30 or higher on their AMPA and have earned the C1 ASI. Familiarization with Army Bands doctrinal publications such as TC 1-19.10, TC 1-19.30, TC 1-19.50, TC 1-19.51, and TC 1-19.52, AR 220-90, and DA PAM 220-90 should begin at this stage.', '1fb4e1a89dc8ebf82983edec06977ad77594652198a6a3d18cb174a5df8a06b8', 17, 17, null, 16, null, null, false, null),
  ('216ee466-817c-5358-a2a0-643fbb961f93', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'credentials', 'E-1..E-4', null, 'Specialist/Corporal — Credentials/Certifications', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Civilian credentialing contributes to military career development and may be accepted for self-development requirements and in performance evaluations. Some credentials are more related to the 42R - Musician MOS while Common Core Credentials are related to duties performed by any Soldier regardless of MOS. Credentials may require additional education, training, or experience. For more information about credentialing opportunities visit the ARMY COOL (Credentialing Opportunities On-Line) website. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.', 'f8ed8baea1cecb057a6d43e0eeba395785c1170d21af5416a4f89e823af65b2f', 17, 17, null, 17, null, null, false, null),
  ('df4fdaff-698b-57f5-936b-a9f2b27f6aa0', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'assignments', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Assignments', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Sergeants are Musicians in an Army Band. In addition to building a strong base of technical expertise in MOS skills and Army Warrior Tasks, Junior NCOs must seek challenging and progressive leadership positions to develop their leadership and supervisory abilities. Musicians in the rank of SGT may serve as Assistant MPT Leaders, Section Leaders, and as Section NCOs in support of Music Support Sections. They may also perform collateral duties such as a Drum Major or Lessons Learned NCO.
(1) Operational Assignments. Assignments include Musician at a General Support or Direct Support Band.
(2) Institutional Assignments. TNG NCO (US Army School of Music.
(3) Broadening Assignments. Musician (Army Musical Outreach); Musician (SHAPE International Band); Drill Sergeant; Recruiter.
(4) Key and Developmental Assignments. Musician', '78398143f4125d068c64274dd0a1407e5db879c70747baf488090d99e7e4a81d', 17, 17, null, 18, null, null, false, null),
  ('1f666971-614a-5b28-8d2f-62fbc62134e2', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'training', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Professional Military Education. Advanced Leader Course (ALC). Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve recognition such as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Commandant’s List, Distinguished Leader Award, or Superior Academic Achievement.
(2) JPME. Enlisted JPME (SEJPME) I (Available through the ATRRS Self Development Center)
(3) Functional Training. Functional courses enhance the effectiveness of military personnel in specific skill areas. These courses do not award an MOS, SQI, or ASI, but the skills are important to overall unit readiness. Examples include CBRNE defense, unit armorer, ammunition handler, combat life saver, DTMS operator, EST operator, field sanitation, unit movement officer, unit load team, HAZMAT transportation, air load planner, container control officer, self-help facilities maintenance, GCSS-A supply maintenance manager, equal opportunity leader (EOL), unit retention NCO, and unit prevention leader (UPL).
(4) Additional Training. Additional skills qualification training. NCOs may be selected for ASI and SQI producing courses that are necessary for overall unit readiness and training or for specific assignments: Master Fitness Trainer (ASI P5), Pregnancy Postpartum PT (P3T) Program Leader (ASI A6), Air Assault (ASI 2B), and Airborne (SQI P).', 'e412b6e903ad69cf4c3a98a7e83ae5f1bd756f8b31d4c0f114ce5c9007d4cc71', 17, 18, null, 19, null, null, false, null),
  ('adab5971-3d36-5705-952c-b0fd6f391bf6', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Civilian Education. Many band members enlist after completing advanced degrees in their musical performance or technical specialty. Continued study in any discipline is highly encouraged regardless of degrees and certifications earned before enlistment.
(a) Soldiers can receive up to $4500 per fiscal year through the Army’s tuition and credentialing assistance programs (https://www.armyignited.army.mil)
(b) The Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) provides no-cost education and career-planning, including college credit by examination or for military training and experiences. https://www.dantes.mil.', '9fcc9f2a5a0b8863bf0a94523d1db277f69c2d5ad265b4c31d6d4c205b136016', 18, 18, null, 20, null, null, false, null),
  ('39a4052a-2769-5548-875e-e3c488e83af2', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'self_development', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Competitive Boards. Competition boards such as NCO of the Quarter/Year and Sergeant Audie Murphy/Sergeant Morales Clubs, broaden the Soldier’s knowledge base, instill discipline and improve the Soldier’s ability to communicate verbally.
(2) Stewardship of the profession. The Center for Army Leadership conducts studies, develops doctrine, provides assessment programs, and develops products and services that improve Army leadership, support developing leaders and strengthen the profession in order to achieve positive command climates and mission ready units. Visit https://usacac.army.mil/Organizations/Centers-of-Excellence-CoE/MCCoE/Center-For-Army- Leadership-CAL/ for self and leader development tools and kits.
(3) Guided. Musicians must work to achieve or exceed MOSQ standards stated in USASOM Regulation 350-70-4 in order to maintain MOS qualification and continually strive to improve their MOS skills to meet the technical and leadership requirements of higher grades. The most qualified Army Musicians should have a 30 or higher on their AMPA and have earned the C1 ASI. Along with Army Regulations and policies, Army Bands doctrinal and leadership publications should begin to inform and guide their thinking and decision making as leaders, trainers, and advisors.', '3c1e81bb83331566b4df3ab0e2e6624d7a9a72d09066128c17e545065448d798', 18, 18, null, 21, null, null, false, null),
  ('9aa3d092-5949-57ac-91b2-566c4deff827', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'credentials', 'E-5', null, 'Sergeant — Credentials/Certifications', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Civilian credentialing contributes to military career development and may be accepted for self-development requirements and in performance evaluations. Some credentials are more related to the 42R - Musician MOS while Common Core Credentials are related to duties performed by any Soldier regardless of MOS. Credentials may require additional education, training, or experience. For more information about credentialing opportunities visit the ARMY COOL (Credentialing Opportunities On-Line) website. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html', 'b8c6a360a30eb36c2c01a244b3a268ea34e5bbe84c23bfd8dcb6e2e6cdba2c0b', 18, 18, null, 22, null, null, false, null),
  ('b172288d-17e1-518f-b16d-ee75a13a4755', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'assignments', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Assignments', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Staff Sergeants primarily serve as Squad Leaders in a Platoon or as Support NCOs in a Band Music Support Section. As a Squad Leader, the NCO organizes, instructs, trains, counsels, and evaluates junior grade Soldiers within the squad. SSGs who are assigned to Army Bands as Support NCOs will supervise the following Music Support Sections: Safety, Security, and Communications section, Public Affairs/Production section; and Training section. SSGs may also serve as NCOs in Music Support Sections, as musicians in an MPT, as Assistant MPT Leaders, or as MPT Leaders. They may also perform duties as Ceremonial Conductor, Drum Major, EO Leader, Master Fitness Trainer, Master Resilience Trainer, Lessons Learned NCO, and Retention NCO as required.
(1) Operational Assignments. Assignments include Squad Leader at a General Support or Direct Support Band; Support NCO at a General Support or Direct Support Band.
(2) Institutional Assignments. USASOM NCOA SGL, Drill Sergeant, Instructor; Band Recruiter (USAREC); Training Developer, Training Support NCO, Operations Sergeant, Supply NCOIC, Supply Clerk.
(3) Broadening Assignments. Musician (SHAPE International Band); Musician (Army Musical Outreach Team); BCT Drill Sergeant; BLC Instructor; DA Select Recruiter; Defense Attaché; Enlisted Aide; Training with Industry; BN/BDE VA.
(4) Key and Developmental Assignments. Squad Leader; Support NCO.', 'be44c25aab2b9ec97225c8723c78c68d92213216da4def0190f66a28eaf38609', 18, 19, null, 23, null, null, false, null),
  ('fd0d1d20-d6ce-5d0d-add3-93c89ca73529', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'training', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Professional Military Education. Senior Leader Course. Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve recognition such as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Commandant’s List, Distinguished Leader Award, or Superior Academic Achievement.
(2) JPME. Enlisted JPME (SEJPME) I (Available through the ATRRS Self Development Center)
(3) Functional Training. Functional courses enhance the effectiveness of military personnel in specific skill areas. These courses do not award an MOS, SQI, or ASI, but the skills are important to overall unit readiness. Examples include CBRNE defense, unit armorer, ammunition handler, combat life saver, DTMS operator, EST operator, field sanitation, unit movement officer, unit load team, HAZMAT transportation, air load planner, container control officer, self-help facilities maintenance, GCSS-A supply maintenance manager, equal opportunity leader (EOL), unit retention NCO, and unit prevention leader (UPL).
(4) Additional skills qualification training. NCOs may be selected for ASI and SQI producing courses that are necessary for overall unit readiness and training or for specific assignments: Master Fitness Trainer/Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator (ASI P5), Pregnancy Postpartum PT (P3T) Program Leader (ASI A6), Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention (SHARP) Level I (ASI 1B), Equal Opportunity Leader Course, Common Faculty Development – Instructor Course (SQI 8), Common Faculty Development – Developer Course (SQI 2), Training & Education Developer Middle Manager Course (TEDMMC), Knowledge Management Qualification (ASI 1E), Air Assault (ASI 2B), and Airborne (SQI P).', '0c19157ce8d79deecf9af2d472aa0ffddbaa622256f443c9e6868f52844be415', 19, 19, null, 24, null, null, false, null),
  ('64bc6fdd-13a0-5f48-9241-2428e5dbad73', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Civilian Education. Many band members enlist after completing advanced degrees in their musical performance or technical specialty. Continued study in any discipline is highly encouraged regardless of degrees and certifications earned before enlistment.
(a) Soldiers can receive up to $4500 per fiscal year through the Army’s tuition and credentialing assistance programs (https://www.armyignited.army.mil)
(b) The Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) provides no-cost education and career-planning, including college credit by examination or for military training and experiences. https://www.dantes.mil.', '9fcc9f2a5a0b8863bf0a94523d1db277f69c2d5ad265b4c31d6d4c205b136016', 19, 19, null, 25, null, null, false, null),
  ('2dec0f26-403b-5708-8062-8cf6e97ff88a', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'self_development', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Self-Development', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Competitive Boards. Competition boards such as NCO of the Quarter/Year and Sergeant Audie Murphy/Sergeant Morales Clubs, broaden the Soldier’s knowledge base, instill discipline and improve the Soldier’s ability to communicate verbally.
(2) Stewardship of the profession. The Center for Army Leadership conducts studies, develops doctrine, provides assessment programs, and develops products and services that improve Army leadership, support developing leaders and strengthen the profession in order to achieve positive command climates and mission ready units. Visit https://usacac.army.mil/Organizations/Centers-of-Excellence-CoE/MCCoE/Center-For-Army- Leadership-CAL/ for self and leader development tools and kits.
(3) Guided. Musicians must work to achieve or exceed MOSQ standards stated in USASOM Regulation 350-70-4 in order to maintain MOS qualification and continually strive to improve their MOS skills to meet the technical and leadership requirements of higher grades. The most qualified Army Musicians should have a 30 or higher on their AMPA and have earned the C1 ASI. SSGs should read publications on the Army and their command team’s professional reading list. Continuous professional reading aids the NCO in developing and refining the organizational leadership skills needed to coach, teach and mentor Soldiers. Publishing articles in professional journals such as the NCO Journal is strongly encouraged.', '3eb7b0389ad2ff19c423ed63dd25fa210bd0a6f3b08b3e798b63e26d3aa493cb', 19, 20, null, 26, null, null, false, null),
  ('d94714a6-ab0e-59ba-823c-ccb563ffada3', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'credentials', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — Credentials/Certifications', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Civilian credentialing contributes to military career development and may be accepted for self-development requirements and in performance evaluations. Some credentials are more related to the 42R - Musician MOS while Common Core Credentials are related to duties performed by any Soldier regardless of MOS. Credentials may require additional education, training, or experience. For more information about credentialing opportunities visit the ARMY COOL (Credentialing Opportunities On-Line) website. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html', 'b8c6a360a30eb36c2c01a244b3a268ea34e5bbe84c23bfd8dcb6e2e6cdba2c0b', 20, 20, null, 27, null, null, false, null),
  ('aa71cd6e-9783-5ff0-8393-a8f9c2fb0f74', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-6', null, 'Staff Sergeant — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(f) Additional guidance. Along with evaluating exceptional performance through achievements, personal awards, recognition, and civilian education, consider the following to identify the most qualified and highly qualified SSGs to be selected to the next higher grade:
(1) Most qualified. A SSG that has successfully completed their key experience positions should be considered ahead of their peers for promotion. The most qualified SSG to be selected for SFC looks like:
(a) Leadership positions. 18+ months highly rated service as Squad Leader, USASOM NCOA SGL or Drill Sergeant. Additional successful service in an institutional or broadening assignment should be considered favorably.
(b) Physical fitness. Overall score of 450 or higher on the AFT with a minimum of 90 points in each event as highlighted on NCOER.
(c) Military Education. ALC - Distinguished Honor Graduate; Distinguished Leadership Award; Honor Graduate; Commandants List.
(d) Civilian Education. Bachelor’s degree complete; Lean Six Sigma Black Belt.
(e) Training. Two or more of the following courses: SHARP (ASI 1B), EO, CFD-IC (SQI 8), CFD-DC (SQI-2), MFT/H2F (ASI P5), P3T (ASI A6).
(f) Other Indicators. Consistently chosen to serve above grade. Continues to seek civilian and military education. Impact awards for outstanding achievements and contributions to the field, Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, has a 30+ AMPA score and/or has the C1 ASI, Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club, Airborne or Air Assault Qualification, and community service activities are additional indicators of NCOs who embrace the Total Soldier Concept and have outstanding potential for service at higher grades.
(2) Highly qualified. A highly qualified SSG to be selected for SFC looks like:
(a) Leadership positions. 18+ months as Squad Leader, Support NCO, or in an institutional or broadening assignment.
(b) Physical fitness. Overall score of 400 or higher on the AFT with a score of 80 in each event.
(c) Military Education. ALC – Commandants List, Exceeded Course Standard, Superior Academic Achievement.
(d) Civilian Education. Bachelor’s degree complete, Lean Six Sigma Green or Yellow Belt.
(e) Training. One of the following courses: SHARP (ASI 1B), EO, CFD-IC (SQI 8), CFD-DC (SQI-2), MFT/H2F Integrator (ASI P5), P3T (ASI A6).
(f) Other Indicators. Continues to seek civilian and military education. Impact awards for outstanding achievements and contributions to the field, Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club, Airborne or Air Assault Qualification, and community service activities are additional indicators of NCOs who embrace the Total Soldier Concept and have outstanding potential for service at higher grades.', '7b2c8448306cdc1055958393b0f24581c1711b8b0c1cfe0422b0c85e31506585', 20, 21, null, 28, null, null, false, null),
  ('9382b10c-1011-5d76-bca1-f15ebaa9ee0c', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'assignments', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Assignments', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Sergeants First Class serve as Platoon Sergeants, Senior Support NCOs, or Operations Sergeants (select Direct Support Units). Platoon Sergeants train and lead Platoons of two to three squads, account for, counsel, train, mentor, and evaluate Squad Leaders, and senior rate Sergeants within their Platoons and others as required. Senior Support NCOs supervise the following Music Support Sections: Human Resources; Logistics and Resource Management; and Training. Operations Sergeants advise the commander on all aspects of band operations, supervise the operations section of the unit in both Direct Support and General Support Units, and serve as quality control alongside the commander. They are responsible for collecting, analyzing, archiving, and disseminating their unit’s best practices and lessons learned from their unit’s operations, experiments, and training events. SFCs may perform duties as MPT Leader, Drum Major, EO Leader, and Holistic Health and Fitness Integrator as required.
(1) Operational Assignments. Operations Sergeant at a Direct Support Band; Platoon Sergeant at a General Support or Direct Support Band; Senior Support NCO at a General Support or Direct Support Band.
(2) Institutional Assignments. USASOM NCOA SGL, Sr Drill Sergeant, Sr Instructor/Instructor; Sr Band Recruiter (USAREC); 42R Talent Management NCO; 42R Career Management NCO; USASOM Director of Ops, AIT Course Manager, Sr Training Developer/Training Developer, Sr Training Support NCO.
(3) Broadening Assignments. Musician or Deputy Director (SHAPE International Band); Musician (Army Musical Outreach Team); BN/BDE EOA, IG NCO, or SARC; BN/BDE VA; BCT Drill Sergeant; Defense Attaché; Enlisted Aide; Training with Industry.
(4) Key and Developmental Assignments. Platoon Sergeant; Senior Support NCO; Operations Sergeant.', 'fccaeef6374c21c083559e765408be5e6c811917c7e3191242cae5b7aa4cb899', 21, 21, null, 29, null, null, false, null),
  ('6b9d5bd8-8f8b-547e-8508-2961bc5cb714', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'training', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Professional Military Education. Master Leader Course. Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve recognition such as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Commandant’s List, Distinguished Leader Award, or Superior Academic Achievement.
(2) JPME. Enlisted JPME (SEJPME) 2 (Available through the ATRRS Self Development Center)
(3) Functional Training. Functional courses enhance the effectiveness of military personnel in specific skill areas. These courses do not award an MOS, SQI, or ASI, but the skills are important to overall unit readiness. Examples include CBRNE defense, unit armorer, ammunition handler, combat life saver, DTMS operator, EST operator, field sanitation, unit movement officer, unit load team, HAZMAT transportation, air load planner, container control officer, self-help facilities maintenance, GCSS-A supply maintenance manager, equal opportunity leader (EOL), unit retention NCO, and unit prevention leader (UPL).
(4) Additional skills qualification training. NCOs may be selected for ASI and SQI producing courses that are necessary for overall unit readiness and training or for specific assignments: Battle Staff NCO (ASI 2S), Company Commander/First Sergeant Course, Sexual Harassment/Assault Response and Prevention (SHARP) Level I (ASI 1B), Equal Opportunity Leader Course, Common Faculty Development – Instructor Course (SQI 8), Common Faculty Development – Developer Course (SQI 2), Training & Education Developer Middle Manager Course (TEDMMC), Knowledge Management Qualification (ASI 1E), Force Management Orientation Course (FMOC), Manpower and Force Management Course (MFMC), Air Assault (ASI 2B), and Airborne (SQI P).', '1955023d1c109921415428906413a241f3de2e06e46cdddcc1dfcddad3411876', 21, 22, null, 30, null, null, false, null),
  ('e51d9b2e-fd6b-52da-ad92-e7abc7f3113b', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'lifelong_learning', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Lifelong Learning', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Civilian Education. Many band members enlist after completing advanced degrees in their musical performance or technical specialty. Continued study in any discipline is highly encouraged regardless of degrees and certifications earned before enlistment.
(a) Soldiers can receive up to $4500 per fiscal year through the Army’s tuition and credentialing assistance programs (https://www.armyignited.army.mil)
(b) The Defense Activity for Non-Traditional Education Support (DANTES) provides no-cost education and career-planning, including college credit by examination or for military training and experiences. https://www.dantes.mil.', '9fcc9f2a5a0b8863bf0a94523d1db277f69c2d5ad265b4c31d6d4c205b136016', 22, 22, null, 31, null, null, false, null),
  ('5489820e-54f8-545f-ac8c-40a60d82ea6c', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'self_development', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Self-Development', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Competitive Boards. Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club.
(2) Stewardship of the profession. The Center for Army Leadership conducts studies, develops doctrine, provides assessment programs, and develops products and services that improve Army leadership, support developing leaders and strengthen the profession in order to achieve positive command climates and mission ready units. Visit https://usacac.army.mil/Organizations/Centers-of-Excellence-CoE/MCCoE/Center-For-Army- Leadership-CAL for self and leader development tools and kits.
(3) Guided. Musicians must work to achieve or exceed MOSQ standards stated in USASOM Regulation 350-70-4 in order to maintain MOS qualification and continually strive to improve their MOS skills to meet the technical and leadership requirements of higher grades. The most qualified Army Musicians should have a 30 or higher on their AMPA and have earned the C1 ASI. Senior NCOs should read publications on the Army and their command team’s professional reading list. Continuous professional reading aids the Senior NCO in developing and refining the organizational leadership skills needed to coach, teach and mentor Soldiers. Publishing articles in professional journals such as the NCO Journal is strongly encouraged.', '6de5465b8297033c6922bdc8290015b22baa3bc1ce0fc940b60ebe327dbf5f2d', 22, 22, null, 32, null, null, false, null),
  ('7f0f74d0-6eae-5572-96d1-76f17214d4db', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'credentials', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — Credentials/Certifications', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Civilian credentialing contributes to military career development and may be accepted for self-development requirements and in performance evaluations. Some credentials are more related to the 42R - Musician MOS while Common Core Credentials are related to duties performed by any Soldier regardless of MOS. Credentials may require additional education, training, or experience. For more information about credentialing opportunities visit the ARMY COOL (Credentialing Opportunities On-Line) website. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.', 'f8ed8baea1cecb057a6d43e0eeba395785c1170d21af5416a4f89e823af65b2f', 22, 22, null, 33, null, null, false, null),
  ('fc36ff0f-b19f-5402-8444-768edebeeb80', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-7', null, 'Sergeant First Class — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(f) Additional guidance. Along with evaluating exceptional performance through achievements, personal awards, recognition, and civilian education, consider the following to identify the most qualified and highly qualified SFCs to be selected to the next higher grade:
(1) Most qualified. A SFC that has successfully completed their key experience positions should be considered ahead of their peers for promotion. The most qualified SFC to be selected for MSG looks like:
(a) Leadership positions. 18+ months highly rated service as Operations Sergeant, Platoon Sergeant, USASOM NCO SGL, or Sr Drill Sergeant. Additional successful service in an institutional or broadening assignment should be considered favorably.
(b) Physical fitness. Overall score of 450 or higher on the AFT with a minimum of 90 points in each event as highlighted on NCOER.
(c) Military Education. SLC - Distinguished Honor Graduate; Distinguished Leadership Award; Commandants List.
(d) Civilian Education. Graduate degree or credential/certification program in progress; Lean Six Sigma Black Belt.
(e) Training. Two or more of the following courses: CCFSPC, Battle Staff (ASI 2S), TEDMMC, KM (ASI 1E), CFD-IC (SQI 8), CFD-DC (SQI 2), Force Management Orientation Course, Manpower Force Management Course.
(f) Other Indicators. Consistently chosen to serve above grade. Continues to seek civilian and military education. Impact awards for outstanding achievements and contributions to the field, Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, has a 30+ AMPA score and/or has the C1 ASI, Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club, Airborne or Air Assault Qualification, and community service activities are additional indicators of NCOs who embrace the Total Soldier Concept and have outstanding potential for service at higher grades.
(2) Highly qualified. A highly qualified SFC to be selected for MSG looks like:
(a) Leadership positions. 18+ months as Operations Sergeant, Platoon Sergeant, Senior Support NCO, or in an institutional or broadening assignment.
(b) Physical fitness. Overall score of 400 or higher on the AFT with a score of 80 in each event.
(c) Military Education. SLC – Commandants List; Exceeded Course Standard; Superior Academic Achievement.
(d) Civilian Education. Graduate degree or credential/certification program in progress; Lean Six Sigma Green or Yellow Belt.
(e) Training. One of the following courses: CCFSPC, Battle Staff (ASI 2S), TEDMMC, KM (ASI 1E), CFD-IC (SQI 8), CFD-DC (SQI 2), Force Management Orientation Course, Manpower Force Management Course.
(f) Other Indicators. Continues to seek civilian and military education. Impact awards for outstanding achievements and contributions to the field, Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club, Airborne or Air Assault Qualification, and community service activities are additional indicators of NCOs who embrace the Total Soldier Concept and have outstanding potential for service at higher grades.', '0d59342c8393a9cf5704af00035ba3b090be99d63e4dff0a1d65f37a5f5268d2', 22, 23, null, 34, null, null, false, null),
  ('65d821f6-0c13-52c3-8ca2-1516ae45a2e9', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'assignments', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant and First Sergeant — Assignments', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Master Sergeants serve as First Sergeants or Operations Sergeants in an Army Band. First Sergeants manage the enlisted musical structure of the organization and the Music Support Section structure of the organization. 1SGs counsel, train, mentor, and rate SFC Musicians and senior rate skill SSG Musicians as required. They serve as the band commander in their absence and are the principal enlisted conductor of the band in Direct Support Units. Operations Sergeants advise the commander on all aspects of band operations, supervise the operations section of the unit in both Direct Support and General Support Units, and serve as quality control alongside the commander. They are responsible for collecting, analyzing, archiving, and disseminating their unit’s best practices and lessons learned from their unit’s operations, experiments, and training events. Operations Sergeants counsel, train, mentor, and rate SFC Musicians and senior rate SSG Musicians as required.
(1) Operational Assignments. Band 1SG; Operations Sergeant.
(2) Institutional Assignments. Army Bands NCOA Deputy Commandant, USASOM 1SG, Sr Band Recruiter (USAREC); USASOM QAE/FSD Director, Training Department NCOIC, Sr Training Support (Chief), Sr Training Support.
(3) Broadening Assignments. Director (SHAPE International Band); BN/BDE EOA, IG NCO, or SARC; MLC Instructor; Enlisted Aide; Training with Industry.
(4) Key and Developmental Assignments. Band 1SG; Band Operations Sergeant.', '5e83c0cfa438041cc1f99645ac5db46692b49958fdf70bb3d6f322383cd8fa93', 23, 24, null, 35, null, null, false, null),
  ('92218508-7be8-5058-9d99-b18022b079d4', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'training', 'E-8', null, 'Master Sergeant and First Sergeant — Institutional Training', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(1) Professional Military Education. Sergeants Major Leader Course. Consider NCOs ahead of their peers and best qualified for promotion if they achieve recognition such as Distinguished Honor Graduate, Commandant’s List, Distinguished Leader Award, or Superior Academic Achievement.
(2) JPME. Enlisted JPME (SEJPME) 2 (Available through the ATRRS Self Development Center)
(3) Functional Training. Functional courses enhance the effectiveness of military personnel in specific skill areas. These courses do not award an MOS, SQI, or ASI, but the skills are important to overall unit readiness. Examples include CBRNE defense, unit armorer, ammunition handler, combat life saver, DTMS operator, EST operator, field sanitation, unit movement officer, unit load team, HAZMAT transportation, air load planner, container control officer, self-help facilities maintenance, GCSS-A supply maintenance manager, equal opportunity leader (EOL), unit retention NCO, and unit prevention leader (UPL).
(4) Additional skills qualification training. NCOs may be selected for ASI and SQI producing courses that are necessary for overall unit readiness and training or for specific assignments: Battle Staff NCO (ASI 2S), Company Commander/First Sergeant Course, Quality Assurance Evaluator Basic Course, Manager Development Course, Commander’s Safety Course, Common Faculty Development – Instructor Course (SQI 8), Common Faculty Development – Developer Course (SQI 2), Force Management Orientation Course (FMOC), Manpower and Force Management Course (MFMC), Air Assault (ASI 2B), and Airborne (SQI P).', 'be0f47b7b79f85d91544200062449c51515bd56689218e4f694d76ad46ff360a', 24, 24, null, 36, null, null, false, null),
