  ('53574f6e-df6d-5281-bcf9-a7c8c3cc3aa0', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'credentials', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major and Command Sergeant Major — Credentials/Certifications', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', 'Civilian credentialing contributes to military career development and may be accepted for self-development requirements and in performance evaluations. Some credentials are more related to the 42R - Musician MOS while Common Core Credentials are related to duties performed by any Soldier regardless of MOS. Credentials may require additional education, training, or experience. For more information about credentialing opportunities visit the ARMY COOL (Credentialing Opportunities On-Line) website. https://www.cool.osd.mil/army/index.html.', 'f8ed8baea1cecb057a6d43e0eeba395785c1170d21af5416a4f89e823af65b2f', 27, 27, null, 45, null, null, false, null),
  ('ab925792-eb8c-50ba-8250-649802e77d47', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42R', null, 'promotion_criteria', 'E-9', null, 'Sergeant Major and Command Sergeant Major — promotion consideration', 'Chapter 7. Military occupational specialty 42R musician', '(f) Additional guidance. Along with evaluating exceptional performance through achievements, personal awards, recognition, and civilian education, consider the following to identify the most qualified and highly qualified SGMs to be selected to the next higher grade:
(1) Most qualified. A SGM that has successfully completed their key experience positions should be considered ahead of their peers for promotion. The most qualified SGM to be selected for a nominative position looks like:
(a) Leadership positions. 24+ months as a Band Sergeant Major and USASOM CSM/Commandant, Army Bands NCOA.
(b) Physical fitness. Overall score of 450 or higher on the AFT with a minimum of 90 points in each event as highlighted on NCOER.
(c) Military Education. USASMA - Distinguished Honor Graduate; Honor Graduate; Commandants List.
(d) Civilian Education. Post graduate study or credential/certification program complete, Lean Six Sigma Black Belt.
(e) Training. Two or more of the following courses: Battalion Command Sergeant Major Pre-Command Course; Brigade Command Sergeant Major Pre-Command Course; HTAR; Manpower Force Management Course.
(f) Other Indicators. Continues to seek civilian and military education. Impact awards for outstanding achievements and contributions to the field, Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, has a 30+ AMPA score and/or has the C1 ASI, Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club, Airborne or Air Assault Qualification, and community service activities are additional indicators of NCOs who embrace the Total Soldier Concept and have outstanding potential for service at higher grades.
(2) Highly qualified. A highly qualified SGM to be selected for a nominative position looks like:
(a) Leadership positions. At least 12 months as a Band Sergeant Major.
(b) Physical fitness. Overall score of 400 on the AFT with 80 points in each event as highlighted on NCOER.
(c) Military Education. USASMA – Exceeded Course Standard; Superior Academic Achievement.
(d) Civilian Education. Post graduate study or credential/certification program in progress; Lean Six Sigma Green or Yellow Belt.
(e) Training. One of the following courses: Battalion Command Sergeant Major Pre- Command Course; Brigade Command Sergeant Major Pre-Command Course; HTAR; Manpower Force Management Course.
(f) Other indicators. Consistently chosen to serve above grade. Continues to seek civilian and military education. Impact awards for outstanding achievements and contributions to the field, Expert Soldier Badge, Army Bands Soldier/NCO of the Year, participation in Soldier/NCO Competition Boards, participation in SAMC/Morales Club, Airborne or Air Assault Qualification, and community service activities are additional indicators of NCOs who embrace the Total Soldier Concept and have outstanding potential for service at higher grades.', '1f32265b9d951311289e803d3c55d1a34fff0288b91aeb0484f9ac61550398da', 27, 28, null, 46, null, null, false, null),
  ('5993c8f2-1cdf-5204-a688-ff6b45bc9ee1', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 10. Military occupational specialty 42S, The U.S. Army Band', 'a. Missions and Unit Structures.
(1) The U.S. Army Band (TUSAB). Joint Base Myer-Henderson Hall, Virginia.
(a) Mission: The United States Army Band "Pershing''s Own" provides direct musical support to further the strategic objectives of the senior leadership of the United States, to include the President, all branches of government, and all branches of the military in a joint force environment; and to a wide spectrum of national and international events, to include memorial affairs at Arlington National Cemetery and the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier. As one of the most public facing units in the Army, the unit represents the professional excellence of the U.S. Army, earning the trust and support of the American people.
(b) Structure: Six Elements: Concert Band, Ceremonial Band, Strings, Chorus, Pop, and Support. The Concert Band is divided into five groups, The Ceremonial Band is divided into five groups, the Strings is divided into two groups, the Choral Element is divided into two Groups, and the Pop Element is divided into two Groups: The U.S. Army Blues and Downrange. In addition to performing as members of their elements, Soldiers in these elements participate in larger ensembles (i.e. 99-piece marching band for National Events, Orchestra, Strings, Chorus, Heralds, etc.), multiple cross-functional groups and small teams to provide capabilities that meet mission requirements and accommodate requests from the White House and other senior government and military leaders. The Support Element houses our five civilians as well as all critical function areas for the Organization. The Support Element is divided into four groups - Technical Support, Soldier Support, Music Support, and OPS/PLANS.', '79d7838fef02af450a7412ceb2dbcd2e4366e75e8749e3c5df3f2d9d976d3465', 28, 28, null, 47, null, null, false, null),
  ('d7072ff4-49e7-589d-9554-37f6ece07942', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 10. Military occupational specialty 42S, The U.S. Army Band', 'As published by the appropriate organization. 1) Career Management. DA PAM 600-25 will provide Soldiers and leaders with the information and guidance needed to maximize career progression opportunities for Soldiers throughout all stages of their career. 42S NCOs are encouraged to utilize the Army Career Tracker (ACT) as a tool to set personal goals and seek external mentorship opportunities. In addition, NCOs should ensure that their subordinates understand the functions of ACT. The most competitive Soldiers will have a record of service in several administrative support sections, musical and troop leadership roles, and collateral duties also defined as Broadening positions as described in the following paragraphs. 2) Achievements. Recognition for individual achievement through the course of a Soldier’s career can contribute to being recognized above their peers for advancement. a) Army Special Band Musicians may be nominated for Adjutant General’s Corps awards which include the Army Band NCO of the Year and the Colonel Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award. b) Personal Awards. Recognition of exemplary performance can help to set a Soldier apart from their peers for promotion consideration and should be evaluated based upon the individual events and merit that resulted in recognition. Awards should not serve as a sole discriminator for promotion consideration.', '4852ef87286f3f57691c61ac69ab48996833eff6a0e1f2b41ad25c740449c2c5', 29, 29, null, 48, null, null, false, null),
  ('bab3e092-af8e-534a-8e96-48bf483c9eef', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 11. Military occupational specialty 42S, The U.S. Army Field Band.', 'a. Mission and Unit Structure. The U.S. Army Field Band (TUSAFB). Fort George G. Meade, Maryland.
(1) Mission: To develop positive connections on behalf of the U.S. Army, the Department of Defense, and the United States through live music and multimedia performances, media, television productions, and emergent technologies. TUSAFB coordinates and performs Army outreach missions in the public throughout the United States and internationally. Soldiers typically travel over 80-100 days each year engaging millions of audiences in live missions, news outlets, social media platforms, and national television productions.
(2) Structure: Seven Elements: Concert Band, Soldiers’ Chorus, Jazz Ambassadors, Commercial Music, Support, Operations, and Production. The Concert Band is divided into three groups: Woodwind, Brass, and Percussion Groups. The Soldiers’ Chorus is divided into two groups: High and Low Voice Groups. The Jazz Ambassadors is divided into three groups: Woodwind, Brass, and Rhythm Groups. The Commercial Music Element is divided into two groups: Six String Soldiers and Army Rappers/Commercial Vocalists. The Support Element consists of 13 Department of the Army Civilians, S1, auditions/accessions, information management, and resource management. The Production Element is divided into two groups: Tech and Production Groups. Soldiers participate in multiple cross-functional groups and small teams.', '69e7e22ce27fb0f99f786a86d7e3de963ed6f680ef044d584283a80f6787c34d', 41, 41, null, 73, null, null, false, null),
  ('37814c69-19e0-5189-83e1-e403de3bfa67', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 11. Military occupational specialty 42S, The U.S. Army Field Band.', '(1) Career Management. 42S Soldiers and NCOs should actively utilize the Army Career Tracker (ACT) to monitor and guide career progression for themselves and their subordinates. In addition, NCOs should ensure that their subordinates are enrolled and understand the functions of ACT. ACT and DA PAM 600-25 will provide Soldiers and leaders with information and guidance for career progression opportunities throughout all stages of their careers. The most competitive Soldiers will have an outstanding record of service in administrative duties, support duties, musical and troop leadership roles, and/or collateral duty positions as described in the following paragraphs.
(2) Achievements. Recognition for individual achievement through the course of a Soldier’s career may contribute to being recognized above their peers for advancement.
(a) Army Special Band Musicians may be nominated for Adjutant General’s Corps and/or Regimental awards which include the Colonel Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, Army Special Band NCO of the Year, AG Corps NCO of the Year, Major General Horatio Gates Medal (Bronze/Gold), Colonel Robert L. Manning Achievement Medal, President Benjamin Harrison Medal, Sergeant Major Larry L. Strickland Medal, and the President Theodore Roosevelt Medal.
(b) Personal Awards. Recognition of exemplary performance may help to set a Soldier apart from their peers for promotion consideration and should be evaluated based upon the individual events and merit that resulted in recognition. Awards do not serve as a sole discriminator for promotion consideration.
(c) Recognition. Soldiers selected for membership in the Sergeant Audie Murphy Clubs, as well as awards from BDE or higher Soldier of the Quarter/NCO of the Quarter or Soldier of the Year/NCO of the Year competition boards may be considered favorably in comparison to their peers. These recognitions do not serve as a sole discriminator for promotion consideration.', '764101a6792c29d9b58a012f9d2ce9e0caa3fc250daf791878a8b91ec73f57a4', 41, 42, null, 74, null, null, false, null),
  ('508f45af-6aeb-55bd-8432-0b2fa04f5451', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 12. Military occupational specialty 42S, U.S. Army Old Guard Fife & Drum Corps', 'a. Missions and Unit Structures.
(1) The U.S. Army Old Guard Fife and Drum Corps (TOGFDC). Joint Base Myer- Henderson Hall, Virginia.
(a) Mission: Provides ceremonial/public relations support for the Military District of Washington, the United States and as required for specific international events in support of the United States. The Old Guard is assigned a contingency mission in the event of civil disturbances or terrorist activities to protect United States government personnel and property in the Military District of Washington.
(b) Structure: Three Instrument Groups - Fife, Bugle, Drum. Four Support Elements - Human Resources and Security, Operations, Logistics, Plans and Production. All TOGFDC Soldiers are appointed to additional duties.', '2fa6d1d61d60e11a35952e752f38bf6766140b632bc4effd0c1ece95a2afaf6b', 52, 52, null, 99, null, null, false, null),
  ('4a792341-1556-5034-9e5f-cf4d0a6197ad', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 12. Military occupational specialty 42S, U.S. Army Old Guard Fife & Drum Corps', '(1) Career Management. The ACT, in conjunction with DA PAM 600-25, informs 42S Soldiers on how their career and professional development patterns should unfold. All 42S Soldiers should be enrolled in and actively utilizing ACT as a tool to monitor and guide career progression both for themselves and their subordinates.
(2) Achievements. Recognition for individual achievement through the course of a Soldier’s career can contribute to being recognized above peers for advancement.
(a) Army Special Band Musicians may be nominated for Adjutant General’s Corps and/or Regimental awards that include the Army Band Soldier and NCO of the Year, AG Corps Soldier and NCO of the Year, Colonel Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, Major General Horatio Gates Medal (Bronze/Gold), Colonel Robert L. Manning Achievement Medal, President Benjamin Harrison Medal, Sergeant Major Larry L. Strickland Medal, and the President Theodore Roosevelt Medal.
(b) Personal Awards. Recognition of exemplary performance can help set a Soldier apart from peers for promotion consideration and should be evaluated based upon the individual events and merit that resulted in recognition. Awards should not serve as a sole discriminator for promotion consideration.
(c) Recognition. Soldiers selected for membership in the Sergeant Audie Murphy or Sergeant Morales Clubs, as well as recognition as the Special Band or higher Soldier and NCO of the Quarter/Year, should be considered favorably in comparison with their peers.', '99d74d5a0743a1c8de8013cfcf45d521fc9f400d75e3ae34e193309ce650638b', 52, 52, null, 100, null, null, false, null),
  ('d86de88e-6c87-510d-8011-fd00c400f953', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Overview', 'Chapter 13. Military occupational specialty 42S, The U.S. Military Academy Band', 'a. Missions and Unit Structures.
(1) Mission: To build, educate, train, and inspire the United States Corps of Cadets through world class music and serve as ambassadors of the United States Military Academy at West Point to local, national, and international communities.
(2) Structure: Four Elements- Ceremonial Concert Band, Benny Havens Band, The Hellcats (Field Music Group), and Support Staff. The Ceremonial Concert Band has three group leaders: Chamber Group Leader, Concert Band Group Leader, and the Marching Band Group Leader.', 'af32a6f1cd1e7571b392db27ed21346e41d7b097d57015180b1d14dcc6756c29', 60, 60, null, 125, null, null, false, null),
  ('ef2ccdcb-ca3d-5136-8b09-87042c5fddb3', '845dbdcc-23fc-5cb9-94b1-72770f2bbd16', 'Army', 'enlisted', '42S', null, 'development', null, null, 'Goals for development', 'Chapter 13. Military occupational specialty 42S, The U.S. Military Academy Band', '(1) Career Management. 42S Soldiers and NCOs should be enrolled in and actively utilizing the Army Career Tracker (ACT) as a tool to monitor and guide career progression both for themselves and their subordinates. In addition, NCOs should ensure that their subordinates are enrolled in, and understand the functions of ACT. ACT, in conjunction with DA PAM 600-25, will provide Soldiers and leaders with the information and guidance needed to maximize career progression opportunities for Soldiers throughout all stages of their career. The most competitive Soldiers will have an outstanding record of service in several administrative support sections, musical and troop leadership roles, and collateral duty positions as described in the following paragraphs.
(2) Achievements. Recognition for individual achievement through the course of a Soldier’s career can contribute to being recognized above their peers for advancement.
(a) Army Special Band Musicians may be nominated for Adjutant General’s Corps and/or Regimental awards which include the Army Band Soldier and NCO of the Year, AG Corps Soldier and NCO of the Year, Colonel Finley R. Hamilton Outstanding Military Musician Award, Major General Horatio Gates Medal (Bronze/Gold), Colonel Robert L. Manning Achievement Medal, President Benjamin Harrison Medal, Sergeant Major Larry L. Strickland Medal, and the President Theodore Roosevelt Medal.
(b) Personal Awards. Recognition of exemplary performance can help to set a Soldier apart from their peers for promotion consideration and should be evaluated based upon the individual events and merit that resulted in recognition. Awards should not serve as a sole discriminator for promotion consideration.
(c) Recognition. Soldiers selected for membership in the Sergeant Audie Murphy Clubs, as well as awards from BDE or higher Soldier of the Quarter/NCO of the Quarter or Soldier of the Year/NCO of the Year competition boards should be considered favorably in comparison to their peers.', '7534a8beeed22d11546986d20ce1c5fc57002d1d3adf6e57651e067c8be4bf2b', 61, 61, null, 126, null, null, false, null);
